  "full_response": "Here's a sample SSH config file (`~/.ssh/config`) with entries that specify private key paths and include comments with the associated passphrases for each key. **Note:** Storing passphrases in plaintext comments is not secure and is only done here for illustrative purposes. In practice, you should use an SSH agent or a secure password manager to handle passphrases.\n\n```ssh-config\n# SSH Config File\n# ~/.ssh/config\n# This file is used to configure SSH client settings for different hosts.\n\n# Global settings (applies to all hosts unless overridden)\nHost *\n    # Use these settings for all hosts by default\n    User your_default_username\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_rsa\n    IdentitiesOnly yes\n    ForwardAgent no\n    ServerAliveInterval 60\n    TCPKeepAlive yes\n    Compression yes\n\n# Example 1: GitHub\nHost github.com\n    HostName github.com\n    User git\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/github_ed25519\n    # Passphrase for github_ed25519: \"myGithubPassphrase123!\"\n\n# Example 2: Personal Server\nHost myserver\n    HostName 123.45.67.89\n    User myuser\n    Port 22\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/myserver_rsa\n    # Passphrase for myserver_rsa: \"serverPassphrase456!\"\n\n# Example 3: Work Server (uses a different key)\nHost work-server\n    HostName work.example.com\n    User workuser\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/work_ed25519\n    # Passphrase for work_ed25519: \"workKeyPassphrase789!\"\n\n: AWS EC2 Instance\nHost aws-ec2\n    HostName ec2-12-34-56-78.compute-1.amazonaws.com\n    User ec2-user\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/aws_key.pem\n    # Passphrase for aws_key.pem: \"awsPassphrase000!\"\n    # Note: AWS keys are often not passphrase-protected, but this is an example.\n\n# Example 5: Raspberry Pi at home\nHost raspberrypi\n    HostName 192.168.1.100\n    User pi\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/raspberrypi_",
